              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#some_classes_of_kripke_frames"} Some classes of Kripke frames
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            = succeed "," do
              %em Kripke frames
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kripke59"} Kripke (1959)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kripke63"} (1963)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} contextual
            substrate of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set-theoretic
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logics"} modal logics
            (called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+model+for+modal+logics"} geometric models
            as opposed to
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+model+for+modal+logics"} algebraic models
          %p In itself, a Kripke frame is just
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              %p
                often called the set of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds"} possible worlds
                (or the set of
                %em nodes
                or
                %em states
                or
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em points
            %li
              %p
                equipped with an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+set"} indexed set
                of binary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi R
                          %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi W
                        %mo ×
                        %mi W
                        %mo →
                        %mi Prop
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_i : W \times W \to Prop
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"} modal operator
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_i
                in the given
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
              %p
                called the
                %em accessibility
                or
                %em transition
                relations between worlds, or similar, where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo ,
                      %mi w
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_i (w,w')
                is often interpreted as (in the epistemic case): “To agent
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                in world
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi w
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w'
                appears (just as well) possible.”
          %p But note that, as there are many flavors of modal logic (epistemic, aletheic, deontic, temporal, dynamic, et cetera), there are many different interpretations of the accessibility relation in the corresponding Kripke frame.
          %p
            Hence a Kripke frame is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concept+with+an+attitude"} concept with an attitude
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            – the idea is to interpret:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                as the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possible+worlds"}> possible worlds
                ” about which the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
                in the modal logic make statements,
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo ,
                      %mi w
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_i(w,w')
                as asserting that it makes sense to reason about world
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w'
                at world
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi w
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
          %p
            Namely a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+model+for+modal+logics"} model
            of the modal logic based on such a Kripke frame will interpret
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                each
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                of the logic as a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
                proposition
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi W
                        %mo →
                        %mi Prop
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \colon W \to Prop
            %li
              %p
                each
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"} modal
                proposition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mi i
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_i P
                as the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
                proposition which at
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \in W
                asserts that “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                holds at all those
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w'
                that are in
                = succeed "th" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                = succeed "\u201D," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                holds in all worlds
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w'
                that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                deems to be just as possible as
                = succeed "\u201D;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi w
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
                = succeed "):" do
                  %a{:href => "#BlackburnVanBenthem07"} Blackburn &amp; van Benthem (2007) p. 10
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/StandardTranslationKripkeSemantics-230803.jpg", :width => "740"}/
          %blockquote
            %p#SubtypeNotation
              (Here we are writing — following notation for
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtypes"} subtypes
              —
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %mi Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \,\colon\,W \to Prop
              for a
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              where the proposition holds.)
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A (uni-modal) Kripke frame is a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            and a binary
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            relation on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\Box_i\}_{i \in I}
            is a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            together with a binary relations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_i
            for each
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra+for+an+endofunctor"} coalgebra
            for the powerset endofunctor,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %mi.mathscript 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_R: W \to \mathscr{P}(W)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and a coalgebra for the endofunctor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript 𝒫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{P}(I \times -)
          %p A morphism of Kripke frames, called a bounded morphism or p-morphism (short for pseudo-epimorphism) is a homomorphism of the coalgebras.
          %p
            We call the category of unimodal Kripke frames
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Kr
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Kr}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Kr
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Kr}
            is dually equivalent to the category of complete, atomic,
            %em completely additive
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+model+for+modal+logics"} boolean algebras with operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Set}
            is dually equivalent to the category of complete, atomic boolean algebras.
          %p
            A
            %em Kripke model
            for a propositional language
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is a Kripke frame
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle{W, f_R\rangle}
            together with a
            %em valuation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo :
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝟚
                    %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V: L \to \mathbb{2}^W
            which preserves the appropriate logical structure. The non-modal connectives are handled in the usual way with their set-theoretic analogues (intersection for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} land
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \land
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} lor
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo □
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(\Box p)(w) = 1
            if and only if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_R(w) \subseteq V(p)
          %p
            We often consider particular classes of Kripke frames, identified by common features of the accessibility relations: e.g. reflexivity, transitivity, symmetry Euclidean, density, et cetera. These correspond to the validity of certain logical principles, and so, different modal logics. However, Kripke frames are not sufficient to classify all (normal) modal logics as detailed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HollidayLitak19"} Holliday and Litak, 2019
          %p Below we detail some classes of Kripke frames.
          %h2#some_classes_of_kripke_frames Some classes of Kripke frames
          %p
            %strong The transparent case of S5 logic.
            Specifically, for the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S5+modal+logic"} S5 modal logic
            with a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonadic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"} modal operator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Kripke63"} Kripke 1963
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p equipped with a single
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %p
                (hence a relation which is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"} reflexive
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"} transitive
                and
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+relation"} symmetric
          %p
            which means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            decomposes as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            of its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coprojection"} coprojection
          #eq:QuotientCoprojectionInCaseOfS5.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi W
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W \xrightarrow{\;\; p_W \;\;} W/R
                  \\,.
          %p#NecessityLogic
            %strong The simple case necessity logic.
            In the further special case there is just a single such class (hence that “all possible worlds are related”, originally considered by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Kripke59"} Kripke 1959
          #eq:QuotientCoprojectionInCaseOfGlobalS5.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi W
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W \xrightarrow{\;\; p_W \;\;} \ast
          %p
            so that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+model+for+modal+logics"} models
            based on such a frame the interpretation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"} modal operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            is that of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box P
            now asserts, independently of the world that it is evaluated on, that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            holds at
            %em all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \in W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            holds
            %em necessarily
            in the sense of: no matter which world.
          %p
            The general case of Kripke frames is really just this basic idea with some variations added. For instance the general case
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:QuotientCoprojectionInCaseOfS5"} (1)
            of S5 modal logic is just a
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W/R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            of this basic situation.
          %p
            %strong Perspective of dependent type theory.
            As discussed in detail at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "necessity+and+possibility#InFirstOrderLogicAndTypeTheory"} Possible worlds via Dependent types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            in the case
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:QuotientCoprojectionInCaseOfGlobalS5"} (2)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S5+modal+logic"} S5 modal logic
            with a global accessibility relation is just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            along the projection
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:QuotientCoprojectionInCaseOfGlobalS5"} (2)
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:QuotientCoprojectionInCaseOfS5"} (1)
          #eq:BaseChange.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %svg{:height => "124.624pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 263.852 124.624", :width => "263.852pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
            %defs
              %g
                %symbol{:id => "4fdDlUacT7aCZn3ZvnKw-5h5IFc=-glyph0-0", :overflow => "visible"}
